  "text": "The user is asking me to explain the causes of ocean tides, distinguishing between lunar and solar gravity, rotation, coastal geography, and the limits of simplified predictions. They've also included a reference note asking me to distinguish observations from assumptions, check units, causal mechanisms, boundaries, and measurement uncertainty.\n\nLet me think through this carefully and rigorously.\n\n## The Physics of Tides\n\n### What we observe (empirical facts):\n- Coastal water levels rise and fall periodically (typically twice daily at most locations, but sometimes once daily)\n- The dominant period is about 12 hours 25 minutes (semidiurnal) - which is half the lunar day (24h 50min)\n- Spring tides (larger ranges) occur near new and full moons\n- Neap tides (smaller ranges) occur near quarter moons\n- Tide timing varies by location and doesn't simply follow the moon's transit\n- Some locations have mixed tides, some diurnal, some semidiurnal\n- Actual measured tides often don't match simple equilibrium predictions\n\n### The gravitational mechanism (distinguishing careful physics from common misconceptions):\n\n**The key insight often missed:** Tides are NOT caused by the Moon \"pulling water toward itself\" in a simple sense",
